      <section class="chapter" id="brief" aria-labelledby="brief-title">
        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">01</span><h2 id="brief-title">The agent brief</h2></div>
        <div class="brief">
          <p class="small-label">Read this first</p>
          <p class="lead">Use 手応え as the central implementation anchor: the right resistance and satisfying response in every sequence, transition, animation, sound, musical passage, action and decision. Make form and function support purposeful engagement together.</p>
          <ul class="brief-points">
            <li>Keep 手応え present at every scale. Ordinary actions deserve it too; scale intensity and space stronger accents so they retain their thrill.</li>
            <li>Reduce interface exposition. Show actual content and useful states early; place explanation where it helps. Reading and storytelling can deserve substantial text.</li>
            <li>Remove unnecessary steps and administration. Preserve context and progress, automate predictable routine work, and make outcomes easy to understand or correct.</li>
            <li>Minimize repetition while preserving the experience's structural integrity. Subtle novelty and a clear sense of direction can keep a necessary sequence moving.</li>
            <li>Give audiovisual choices and game structures a purposeful role. Use challenge and support to invite initiative; choose density and aesthetics for the actual audience.</li>
            <li>Keep product states truthful and recovery clear. Establish a playable representative sequence early; grill, map, prototype and reflect as the design develops.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="brief-footer">These are strong preferences with reasons behind them. Apply them alongside the current project brief and its actual requirements. The examples below illustrate ways of thinking; you have room to propose a better approach.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="immersion" aria-labelledby="immersion-title">
        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">02</span><h2 id="immersion-title"><span lang="ja">手応え</span>: the central implementation anchor</h2></div>
        <p class="lead">I use 手応え (tegotae) for the right amount of resistance a person feels, making the experience satisfying in both form and function.</p>
        <blockquote>“手応え should NOT be a reward for enduring the mundane, it should always be present and scaled accordingly.”<cite>BIJAN</cite></blockquote>
        <div class="body-copy">
          <p>Build that feeling into the relationship between an intention, an action and what follows. Attend to the whole sequence: how it begins, how it responds, what changes and how it carries someone onward. An ordinary selection deserves the same care as a difficult decision, expressed at an appropriate intensity.</p>
          <p>A precise, almost effortless response can carry 手応え. Space especially thrilling moments so they retain their charge, while sustaining the feeling through the quieter actions between them. An experience can be responsive and still have weight; choose resistance and duration for what the moment asks someone to do.</p>
          <p>Satisfaction can include struggle, unease, disappointment or unresolved tension. These can be meaningful signals that invite someone to take initiative. Let the person perceive what their action means and how they might proceed, even when the intended feeling is uncomfortable or the outcome remains open.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="purpose-contexts" aria-label="Illustrative expressions of 手応え at different scales">
          <div><h3>An ordinary action</h3><p>A selected item responds precisely and settles into its new state. The change has just enough presence to feel deliberate and complete.</p></div>
          <div><h3>A consequential choice</h3><p>The alternatives carry perceptible stakes. Committing changes the situation, giving the person something meaningful to respond to.</p></div>
          <div><h3>A sustained sequence</h3><p>Necessary steps retain continuity, with small developments and a clear direction. The person can feel how each contributes to the purpose.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="body-copy" style="margin-top:23px">
          <h3>Purpose gives the feeling its direction</h3>
          <p>Immersion remains a priority. Every immersive element should connect to the essence of the experience and purpose of the project. Find what someone came to feel, understand or do, and shape the resistance and response around that. In a practical tool, that may mean completing real work with clarity and momentum. In an interactive world, atmosphere and meaningful choices may carry it.</p>
          <p>Features are means to a purposeful end. Discourage needless, aimless behavior by making meaningful activity carry the experience forward. Exploration and rehearsal can contribute to that purpose. Choose game structures when they serve the project; an ambitious cinematic sequence or playful interaction can be exactly right. Reject cheap, purposeless gimmicks. Subtlety and restraint remain creative choices.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="question">What does this action let the person feel, and where does that feeling lead?</p>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="language" aria-labelledby="language-title">
        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">03</span><h2 id="language-title">Show the experience. Explain in context.</h2></div>
        <p class="lead">Too much interface text pulls attention away from the thing someone came to experience.</p>
        <div class="body-copy">
          <p>Give people the actual content, scene, or workspace as early as the activity reasonably allows. Use composition, useful visual relationships, and clear interaction to establish what matters. Keep labels concise. Put supporting detail close to the moment when it becomes useful.</p>
          <p>Distinguish the content from the commentary wrapped around it. A story, reference text, or reading task may need many words. A screen that repeatedly explains its own controls usually needs better design. Preserve instructions that make an action understandable or prevent a consequential misunderstanding.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="split" style="margin-top:25px">
          <div><h3>Let the interface carry meaning</h3><p>A visible current state, a well-placed control, or a diagram may answer a question before anyone has to read an explanation. Use text for the meaning those elements cannot communicate well.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Make depth available</h3><p>Contextual explanation, reference material, and advanced detail can sit within reach. The main path should remain understandable without requiring a tour of everything the product can do.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note"><p><strong>A useful editing test:</strong> identify what each piece of interface copy helps someone decide or do. Cut repetition and replace avoidable explanation with clearer behavior. Keep words that contribute directly to the experience.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="visuals" aria-labelledby="visuals-title">
        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">04</span><h2 id="visuals-title">Think visually, with intent</h2></div>
        <p class="lead">Thoughtful visuals should do real work, including the emotional work of making a project feel like itself.</p>
        <div class="body-copy">
          <p>A visual can reveal a relationship, establish a place, express progress, create anticipation, or make an unfamiliar idea tangible. Choose its form around that role. Illustration, diagrams, typography, photography, spatial composition, and visual interaction offer different ways to serve the subject.</p>
          <p>Give the project a coherent visual character. I am comfortable with substantially different aesthetics across projects. You can choose the palette, typography, shape language, layout, and degree of visual richness. Make those choices together, with the subject and audience in mind.</p>
          <p>Watch for a layout that could belong to almost any product after replacing the headings. Familiar components can work well; choose them because they organize this content or support this interaction. There is no blanket ban on cards, dashboards, sidebars, or any other component.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="question">What does this visual choice help someone perceive or feel that the experience needs?</p>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="motion" aria-labelledby="motion-title">
        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">05</span><h2 id="motion-title">Compose time, motion, sound and music</h2></div>
        <p class="lead">Shape 手応え through timing, movement and listening. The form of the response should make sense with what actually happens.</p>
        <ul class="principle-list">
          <li><strong>Keep up with the user.</strong><p>Every input deserves a prompt, considered response. Tune its onset, movement and settling so even a small action has presence. Scale that presence to the action and the surrounding rhythm.</p></li>
          <li><strong>Make change intelligible.</strong><p>Motion can show where something went, how two states relate, or what an action affected. Its timing should help someone follow that relationship.</p></li>
          <li><strong>Let important moments breathe.</strong><p>Slower pacing can create anticipation, weight or narrative presence. Give a stronger accent room around it; sustain quieter 手応え between accents. Let routine tasks keep their natural working rhythm.</p></li>
          <li><strong>Compose the audiovisual response.</strong><p>Connect sound to the action, environment or story. Let music develop the sequence's direction, tension or release. Coordinate its phrasing, entrances and silence with movement and consequence so the parts feel like one experience.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="body-copy"><p>Stillness and silence can carry 手応え through anticipation, attention or the clarity of a changed state. Choose them deliberately. Choose the channels each moment needs.</p><p>Review the complete sequence in playback and through interaction. Inspect response to input, continuity, musical phrasing, sound alignment and the effect of repetition under realistic conditions. A polished still image cannot establish whether the timing or resistance feels right.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="flow" aria-labelledby="flow-title">
        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">06</span><h2 id="flow-title">Keep the person inside the activity</h2></div>
        <p class="lead">Keep a sense of direction alive through ordinary work. Reduce repetition as far as possible while preserving structural integrity.</p>
        <div class="body-copy">
          <p>Question repeated data entry, redundant confirmation, manual relaying of information and journeys through separate screens to perform one coherent action. Preserve context between steps and make the next useful action clear. Keep distinctions and steps that the experience needs to remain coherent and correct.</p>
          <p>When repetition is necessary, let each pass carry some development. Even a barely perceptible variation can help preserve the feeling, alongside a strong sense of direction and purpose. Choose novelty that belongs to the content or changing state, while keeping familiar controls dependable.</p>
          <p>Use information density to support the activity. A compact workspace may keep everything needed for a task visible. A scene may benefit from having very little interface on top of it. Whitespace and minimalism earn their place through their effect on focus and use.</p>
          <p>Keep frequent actions within practical reach. Secondary tools can appear in context, provided people can find them when needed. Adapt the interaction to the device and setting; investigate the actual use before deciding which platform should lead the design.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="note"><p><strong>Keep effort purposeful:</strong> an extra step can be worthwhile when it improves understanding, creates an intentional experience or gives appropriate control over a consequential action. Tie rewarding responses to meaningful activity. Introduce constraints only when the experience itself gives them a purpose; preserve the person's ability to act.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="logic" aria-labelledby="logic-title">
        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">07</span><h2 id="logic-title">Product logic should preserve continuity</h2></div>
        <p class="lead">The product should carry context and routine work forward, with outcomes people can understand and correct.</p>
        <div class="body-copy"><p>Design defaults, persistence, automation, and failure behavior as part of the experience. A visually convincing interface can still interrupt engagement if it loses progress, invents certainty, or creates a new queue of things to supervise.</p></div>
        <table class="logic-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Concern</th><th scope="col">Preferred direction</th><th scope="col">Keep in view</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Context &amp; progress</td><td>Remember useful choices and preserve meaningful progress. Carry relevant information into the next part of the task.</td><td>What someone needs to resume without rebuilding their place.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Defaults &amp; automation</td><td>Use sensible defaults and automate predictable routine steps. Avoid asking people to supply information the product already has.</td><td>The result of the automation and a practical way to correct it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Consequential actions</td><td>Match the degree of user control to the consequence. Make the effect understandable before commitment where that matters.</td><td>The person's actual ability to correct or recover.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Truthful states</td><td>Distinguish pending, uncertain, failed, and completed outcomes when the difference affects what happens next.</td><td>An attempted action may still lack a confirmed result.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Failure &amp; recovery</td><td>Preserve the work and make the next useful action clear. Where people share responsibility, identify who owes that action.</td><td>Unresolved work, relevant history, and original commitments.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="body-copy" style="margin-top:20px"><p>For example, a sent request, a delivered request, an acknowledgement, and a resolution can require different next actions. Use distinctions that matter in the project. Avoid adding a complicated state system simply because these examples name several states.</p></div>
        <div class="note">
          <h3>Calibrate challenge and support with ZPD</h3>
          <p>Vygotsky's zone of proximal development distinguishes what a learner can do independently from what they can accomplish with guidance or capable peers. <a href="https://home.fau.edu/musgrove/web/vygotsky1978.pdf#page=24">Source: <em>Mind in Society</em>, 1978, pp. 85–86.</a></p>
          <p style="margin-top:12px">As a design approach, offer a meaningful next action within reach with appropriate support. Demonstration, a useful cue or collaboration may help someone take initiative; adjust the support as their capability develops. This is an application of ZPD to the design, to be tested with the intended audience. Let mastered actions stay fluent, and use challenge where learning or growth serves the project.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="context" aria-labelledby="context-title">
        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">08</span><h2 id="context-title">The same preferences, different expressions</h2></div>
        <div class="body-copy"><p>These are possible applications of the preferences. Use them to understand the reasoning, then develop an approach suited to the actual project.</p></div>
        <article class="example">
          <div class="example-title"><p>Illustrative direction</p><h3>An immersive learning experience</h3></div>
          <div class="example-body"><p>Let the learner enter a meaningful situation early. A scene, conversation or simulation can carry the lesson. Offer a challenge they can approach with suitable guidance, and let their attempt change the situation. Use visual and auditory cues to help them notice what that change means.</p><p>A cinematic passage may establish mood or context. A response to the learner's action may need to feel immediate. Give those moments different timing and intensity. When practice is needed, vary the situation enough to make the next attempt purposeful while preserving the skill being practiced.</p><p class="reason">The learning content and the way it is experienced reinforce each other. The learner can remain engaged while receiving enough guidance to participate.</p></div>
        </article>
        <article class="example">
          <div class="example-title"><p>Illustrative direction</p><h3>A reading and reference tool</h3></div>
          <div class="example-body"><p>Give the text a comfortable reading surface. Let a reference open with a clear relationship to its source, and return the reader to the exact passage they left. That ordinary exchange can have 手応え through precise continuity and response. Keep navigation, retrieval and useful references close.</p><p>A phone and a desktop may need different arrangements for the same reference material. Choose the arrangement from the available space and the actual reading behavior.</p><p class="reason">Here, substantial text is the content. The opportunity to reduce text lies in repeated interface explanation, while the design protects continuity through the material.</p></div>
        </article>
        <article class="example">
          <div class="example-title"><p>Illustrative direction</p><h3>A shared coordination workflow</h3></div>
          <div class="example-body"><p>Show what requires attention, who owes the next action and the relevant timing. Let an action visibly change the working situation: an assignment gains an owner, or a resolved item clears the next step. Carry information forward so one person does not become a manual messenger between everyone else.</p><p>If a notification fails, keep delivery trouble distinguishable from a response that has not arrived. If someone acknowledges a problem, preserve the outstanding action until it is actually resolved.</p><p class="reason">A clear shared state reduces administrative work and protects trust. The visual treatment can be expressive while keeping the working information easy to perceive.</p></div>
        </article>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="judgment" aria-labelledby="judgment-title">
        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">09</span><h2 id="judgment-title">Use your own creative judgment</h2></div>
        <p class="lead">I want you to have a point of view. Give the project a considered design, including ideas I would not have specified myself.</p>
        <div class="body-copy">
          <p>You have room to choose a visual identity, layout, interaction model, navigation, density, and audiovisual treatment. Find opportunities the brief leaves open. Familiar conventions and unexpected approaches are both available to you; assess how well they serve the experience.</p>
          <p>Prioritize the intended audience when its needs conflict with my personal taste. For a personal tool, my preferences have direct weight because I am the audience. For a broader audience, test assumptions about what helps those people engage with the project.</p>
          <p>Understand a preference's purpose before applying it mechanically. You may challenge an assumption or depart from a default when you have a concrete reason grounded in the audience, the task, or evidence from trying the experience. Explain a meaningful tradeoff briefly, in terms of its effect.</p>
        </div>
        <h3 style="margin-top:26px">Synthesize the feeling through iteration</h3>
        <div class="body-copy"><p>Establish a playable, representative sequence early, before extending a design widely. Use a rigorous combination of grill-me, Wayfinder, prototype and retro to discover and refine its 手応え. Each method contributes to the same iteration; keep its record in the project's existing decision map or checkpoint.</p></div>
        <div class="reading-path" aria-label="The four-skill design iteration">
          <div><span>GRILL-ME</span><p>Expose missing decisions about the purpose, intended feeling and consequences.</p></div>
          <div><span>WAYFINDER</span><p>Map uncertainty and dependencies. Carry accepted decisions into the next experiment.</p></div>
          <div><span>PROTOTYPE</span><p>Make a representative sequence playable. Test its resistance, response and direction.</p></div>
          <div><span>RETRO</span><p>After each iteration, compare intent with experience and preserve the lessons for the next pass.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="body-copy"><p>Show the sequence working. Try ordinary actions, consequential moments and repeated use, including recovery where it matters. Assess whether the response stays satisfying and the purpose stays perceptible. Record what was demonstrated, what was accepted and what still needs to be tried. Resolve routine choices yourself; bring consequential uncertainty to the user with concrete evidence.</p></div>
        <div class="note"><p><strong>What stays open:</strong> these preferences do not establish a universal palette, font, corner radius, component library, level of minimalism, device priority, or cinematic treatment. Project-specific audience, platform, and delivery requirements still apply. Work them into a coherent experience.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="review" aria-labelledby="review-title">
        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">10</span><h2 id="review-title">Review the experience people actually get</h2></div>
        <p class="lead">Judge the design in use, with representative content and a meaningful journey through it.</p>
        <div class="body-copy"><p>Use these questions to direct attention. Their relevance and relative weight depend on the project; satisfying each sentence mechanically does not establish a good experience.</p></div>
        <ol class="review-list">
          <li>Does 手応え remain present in ordinary actions as well as decisive moments, with resistance and response scaled appropriately?</li>
          <li>Can someone enter the real activity promptly? Does each immersive element help them pursue its purpose?</li>
          <li>Do visuals carry understanding or feeling that the experience needs? Where is text doing work the design could handle better?</li>
          <li>Does the sequence feel right in use and playback, including transitions, animation, sound and music? Are stronger accents given room while quieter moments retain substance?</li>
          <li>Have repetition and administration been minimized without compromising structural integrity? Do necessary repetitions retain enough development and direction to carry someone onward?</li>
          <li>Does the next action invite purposeful initiative, with challenge and support suited to the person? Does the chosen density keep useful actions within reach?</li>
          <li>Does the product preserve context and progress, show honest outcomes, and provide a clear path after a failure?</li>
          <li>Does the design suit the actual audience? Has a representative sequence been tried, with decisions synthesized through grilling, wayfinding, prototyping and a retro after each iteration?</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="body-copy" style="margin-top:23px"><p>Verify the parts that matter directly: rendered layouts, real content, interactions, playback, timing, and relevant device behavior. Use technical checks for what they can establish, and direct inspection for the qualities people will experience.</p></div>
      </section>

      <footer class="footer">
        <p>BIJAN / DESIGN PREFERENCES<br>VERSION 2 · 22 OCTOBER 2026</p>
        <p class="footer-note">Scope: practical software and creative experiences, including UX, UI, and product behavior. Technical choices belong here when they affect that experience; general coding and agent-management rules belong in their own instructions.</p>
      </footer>
    </main>
  </div>
  <script>
    const copyButton = document.getElementById('copy-guide');
    const copyStatus = document.getElementById('copy-status');
    copyButton.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      const guideText = document.title + '\n\n' + document.getElementById('guide').innerText;
      try {
        let copied = false;
        if (navigator.clipboard && window.isSecureContext) {
          try { await navigator.clipboard.writeText(guideText); copied = true; } catch (_) {}
        }
        if (!copied) {
          const buffer = document.createElement('textarea');
          buffer.value = guideText;
          buffer.style.cssText = 'position:fixed;left:-9999px;top:0;';
          document.body.appendChild(buffer);
          try { buffer.select(); copied = document.execCommand('copy'); }
          finally { buffer.remove(); copyButton.focus(); }
        }
        if (!copied) throw new Error('Clipboard unavailable');
        copyStatus.textContent = 'Full guide copied.';
      } catch (_) {
        copyStatus.textContent = 'Select the guide text and copy manually.';
      }
    });
    document.getElementById('print-guide').addEventListener('click', () => window.print());
    const chapterLinks = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('.toc a'));
    const chapters = chapterLinks.map(link => document.querySelector(link.getAttribute('href')));
    if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
      const visibleChapters = new Set();
      const updateCurrent = () => {
        const active = chapters.find(chapter => visibleChapters.has(chapter.id));
        chapterLinks.forEach(link => {
          if (active && link.hash === '#' + active.id) link.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location');
          else link.removeAttribute('aria-current');
        });
      };
      const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
        entries.forEach(entry => entry.isIntersecting ? visibleChapters.add(entry.target.id) : visibleChapters.delete(entry.target.id));
        updateCurrent();
      }, { rootMargin: '-64px 0px -35% 0px' });
      chapters.forEach(chapter => observer.observe(chapter));
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
__BIJAN_EOF__

cat > "$dir/.bijan-block.tmp" <<'__BIJAN_EOF__'
<!-- bijan-design-principles:start -->
# Bijan's design principles

Before any design, UX, UI, product-logic or creative-experience decision, read `~/.claude/bijan-design-principles.html` in full (the text; the CSS is presentation). It is the complete guide and its reasoning governs the work.

## Working terms
- **手応え** in every action: considered, satisfying interaction with an immediate functional response. Scale the care to how much the action matters.
- Aim for purposeful immersion and fluent mastery.
- Resolve routine, reversible choices yourself.
- Bring consequential uncertainty to Bijan with options and a recommendation.
- Build a representative playable sequence early, before polishing parts.
- Keep administration to what the work needs.
- Report in two columns: what was technically verified, and what Bijan has experienced and accepted.

## Tooling
- Run design retros inline: compare the outcome against the intent. (The /retro skill reviews the agent's setup, a different question.)
- Reserve /wayfinder for multi-session work.
<!-- bijan-design-principles:end -->
__BIJAN_EOF__

